# MDC Rule: WordPress/PHP Documentation Best Practices

- Use PHPDoc blocks for all classes, functions, and methods.
- Document all parameters, return types, and exceptions.
- Provide usage examples for public APIs.
- Keep documentation up to date with code changes.
- Use clear, concise language and avoid ambiguity.
- Document all hooks (actions and filters) with purpose, parameters, and return values.
- Add inline comments for complex logic.
- Include proper plugin/theme headers for WordPress recognition.

## Examples

```php
// Hook documentation
/**
 * Fires after saving plugin settings.
 *
 * @param array $settings The saved settings.
 * @return void
 */
do_action( 'my_plugin_after_save_settings', $settings );
```

```php
// Inline comment for complex logic
// Calculate the total price including tax
$total = $subtotal * ( 1 + $tax_rate );
```

```php
// Plugin header
/*
Plugin Name: My Plugin
Description: Example plugin.
Version: 1.0.0
Author: Your Name
Text Domain: my-plugin
*/
```

This rule applies to all *.php files in all subdirectories.







